<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freetown</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Freetown is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, on the Sierra Leone Peninsula at the mouth of the Sierra Leone River. It was founded in 1792 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone-company">Sierra Leone Company</a> as a settlement for freed slaves, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, after the earlier Province of Freedom collapsed. It became the capital of the British colony in 1808 and a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freetown.jpg" alt="Freetown" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Hond · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freetown-aerialview.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> estuary has been inhabited for millennia, and the earliest known group was the Bullom people. In the sixteenth century the Mane people invaded and established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, integrating with the Bullom. In 1787 some 400 formerly enslaved black people sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-for-the-relief-of-the-black-poor">Committee for the Relief of the Black Poor</a> founded the Province of Freedom and the settlement of Granville Town on land bought from the Koya Temne subchief King Tom and the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naimbana">Naimbana</a>. Some historians question how well the Koya leaders understood the agreement, since they had a different conception of property.</p><p>After this colony collapsed, the directors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone-company">Sierra Leone Company</a> were eager to recruit new settlers, and Lieutenant John Clarkson, an abolitionist, was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> to register emigrants. Freetown was founded on 11 March 1792 by 1,196 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> under Clarkson at the foot of the Cotton Tree, as a settlement for free and freed African American, Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and Liberated African slaves, whose descendants are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> people.</p><p>The Temne attacked the colony in 1801 and were repulsed. The British made Freetown a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-colony">Crown Colony</a> in 1808, and from 1808 to 1874 it was the capital of British West Africa. It was also the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s Eastern Squadron, which enforced the ban on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> and brought most of the slaves it freed from trading ships to Freetown. Fourah Bay College, the oldest university in West Africa, was founded there in 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Freetown is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, a major port on the Atlantic with a harbour in the estuary of the Sierra Leone River. The Krio language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> people is by far the most widely spoken language in the city. Landmarks of its founding include the Cotton Tree, King's Yard Gate, whose inscription declares any slave who passes through it free, and the ruins of the slave fortress on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunce-island">Bunce Island</a>, a boat ride away. St George's Cathedral opened in 1828.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Freetown” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Black Christians took heart from the verse of the Psalms “Ethiopia shall soon stretch out her hands unto God”, and some settled in Sierra Leone, where <b>Freetown</b> was founded in 1792, or in Liberia, colonised from 1822 by the American Colonization Society and independent from 1847.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1866 Britain revived the scheme as the West Africa Settlements, with a governor-in-chief at <b>Freetown</b> over Sierra Leone, the Gambia, the Gold Coast and Lagos, each with an administrator.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-west-africa">British West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1787 King Tom granted land to British abolitionists for the Province of Freedom, and King Naimbana confirmed the grant in 1788; the settlement grew into <b>Freetown</b>, though disputes over the terms led to conflict.</blockquote><a href="/state/koya-temne-kingdom">Koya Temne kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The survivors regrouped, and the Sierra Leone Company took over the project, founding the Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate at <b>Freetown</b> in 1792.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-freedom">Province of Freedom</a></li><li><blockquote>After the failure of the Province of Freedom, the Sierra Leone Company brought about 1,100 Black Loyalists from Nova Scotia under John Clarkson, who founded <b>Freetown</b> in 1792.</blockquote><a href="/state/sierra-leone-colony-and-protectorate">Sierra Leone Colony and Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>He bought repeating rifles in <b>Freetown</b>, paid for with gold, ivory and slaves, and had local smiths copy them.</blockquote><a href="/state/wassoulou-empire">Wassoulou Empire</a></li></ul></section>
